      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "BLK_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_DEB95734_CONCEPTS_01_MEASUREMENT_AND_UNITS",
          "semanticAnchorId": "ANCHOR_MEASUREMENT_AND_UNITS",
          "title": "1. Physical Measurement and Definition of Units",
          "contentMarkdown": "Measurement of any physical quantity involves comparison with an internationally accepted reference standard called a **unit**. The result of a measurement is expressed as $Q = n \\cdot u$, where $n$ is the numerical value and $u$ is the unit. Units for fundamental quantities are called **base units**, while combinations of base units yield **derived units**.",
          "boundAtomicFactIds": [
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_DEB95734_DA7F84B0",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_DEB95734_BABBF96B",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_DEB95734_C6745CDC",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_DEB95734_CF2392A3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "BLK_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_DEB95734_CONCEPTS_02_SYSTEMS_AND_SI_STANDARD",
          "semanticAnchorId": "ANCHOR_SYSTEMS_AND_SI_STANDARD",
          "title": "2. Historical Systems and the International System of Units (SI)",
          "contentMarkdown": "Three historical systems were extensively used: CGS (cm, g, s), FPS (foot, pound, s), and MKS (m, kg, s). In 1971, the General Conference on Weights and Measures (CGPM) established the **Systeme Internationale d' Unites (SI)** based on seven fundamental base units:\n1. Length: metre ($\\text{m}$)\n2. Mass: kilogram ($\\text{kg}$)\n3. Time: second ($\\text{s}$)\n4. Electric Current: ampere ($\\text{A}$)\n5. Thermodynamic Temperature: kelvin ($\\text{K}$)\n6. Amount of Substance: mole ($\\text{mol}$)\n7. Luminous Intensity: candela ($\\text{cd}$)",
          "boundAtomicFactIds": [
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_DEB95734_48859D68",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_DEB95734_5C39C275",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_DEB95734_636A1E20",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_DEB95734_5EEFF483",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_DEB95734_E9C529E2",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_DEB95734_18518A52",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_DEB95734_B28DADA1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "BLK_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_DEB95734_CONCEPTS_03_SUPPLEMENTARY_ANGLES",
          "semanticAnchorId": "ANCHOR_SUPPLEMENTARY_ANGLES",
          "title": "3. Supplementary SI Units: Plane Angle and Solid Angle",
          "contentMarkdown": "SI defines two dimensionless supplementary units:\n- **Plane Angle ($d\\theta$)**: measured in radians ($\\text{rad}$), defined as arc length divided by radius ($d\\theta = ds/r$).\n- **Solid Angle ($d\\Omega$)**: measured in steradians ($\\text{sr}$), defined as intercepted area divided by radius squared ($d\\Omega = dA/r^2$).",
          "boundAtomicFactIds": [
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_DEB95734_3B4860F9"
          ]
        }
      ],
